# rich-readable

An **instruction**, not a CSS class or a page badge. Write study HTML so it is scan-first: a reader should catch key words and the decision in a few seconds.

Do **not** wrap content in `.rich-readable`. Do **not** use mermaid — it reads as a sketch, not a study page. Use the helpers already in the app.

## Build order

1. **Chips** — `.chips` / `.chip` for the keywords of the page
2. **Tldr** — `.tldr`, one or two sentences, bold the claim
3. **Split prose** — long paragraphs become `.tight` bullets
4. **Highlight** — `<b>`, `<code>`, `<span class="tag">` on terms that must pop
5. **Boxes** — `.scale` > `.pan` for comparisons; `.note` for a trap; `.callout` / `.callout.tradeoff` for a scored line
6. **Flow** — `<pre class="flow">` box-drawing / ASCII (same as the rest of the hub). Caption with `.diagram-cap` if needed
7. **See also** — `<a class="chip" href="#id">` or `#topic/id` / `#qa/id`

## Color roles (existing classes)

- `.go` — keep / do this
- `.warn` — trade-off
- `.trap` — don’t

```html
<div class="chips"><span class="chip">keyword</span></div>
<div class="tldr"><b>In one line:</b> the claim.</div>
<div class="scale">
  <div class="pan go"><h4>Keep</h4><ul class="tight"><li>…</li></ul></div>
  <div class="pan warn"><h4>Trade-off</h4></div>
  <div class="pan trap"><h4>Trap</h4></div>
</div>
<pre class="flow">producer  →  bounded queue  →  consumer</pre>
```

Walls of prose and mermaid flowcharts are out of spec.
